-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/67a71b1d-a985-5ff1-b3ba-459af8b62b3d
-- title:
--   Relative Jacobian of X₀(p) over ℤ_{(ℓ)}, Abel–Jacobi normalised
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $R$ denotes the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt ℓ`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of the rationals whose denominator is coprime to $\ell$. Fixed data: a natural number $p$ with $p \neq 0$, a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid p$, a scheme $X$ together with a morphism $c \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ that is proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and a section $\varepsilon$ of $c$ (an element of `SchemeHomOver (𝟙 (Spec R)) c`, i.e. a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R \to X$ whose composite with $c$ is the identity).
--
--   The modular function fields are those of the development: `modularFunctionFieldFull p` is the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subset \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the divisor expansions at level $p$; `modularFunctionFieldBar p` is its base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `modularFunctionFieldFullC K p` is the analogous field over a field $K$. Correspondingly `JZero p` is $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \mathtt{modularFunctionFieldBar } p)$, the group of degree-zero divisor classes (degree-zero divisors modulo principal ones) of that function field, and `JZeroC K p` is $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K, \mathtt{modularFunctionFieldFullC } K\, p)$. A `CurveModel K L` is a scheme $C$ with an integral structure and a proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} K$, a ring isomorphism of $L$ with the function field of $C$ compatible with the structure map, a bijection `placeOfPoint` from the closed points of $C$ onto the places of $L$ over $K$ matching stalks with valuation subrings, and the property that every finite set of points of $C$ lies in an affine open; for algebraically closed $K$, `pointEquivPlace` is the induced bijection between sections of $C \to \operatorname{Spec} K$ and places.
--
--   The hypotheses fall into five groups.
--
--   (i) Geometric generic fibre. A curve model $M_\eta$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar p` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, an isomorphism $e_\eta$ from $M_\eta.C$ to the fibre product of $c$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the inclusion $R \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the compatibility `heη` that $e_\eta$ followed by the second projection is $M_\eta$'s structure morphism, and the Galois compatibility `hgal`: for every $g \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and all sections $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$, if $x'$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec} g$ followed by $x$, $e_\eta$ and the first projection, then $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x' = \mathtt{arithmeticGalois}(\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldFull } p)(g) \cdot M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$, the action being that of the semilinear automorphism attached to $g$ on places.
--
--   (ii) Integral models over valuation rings above $\ell$. For each valuation subring $A \subset \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $A.\mathrm{LiesOverPrime}\ \ell$ (that is, $\ell$ is a non-unit of $A$): a ring homomorphism $\rho_A \colon R \to A$, with `hρ` stating that composing $\rho_A$ with the inclusion $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ gives the structure map $R \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; a curve model $M_{s,A}$ of `modularFunctionFieldFullC (ResidueField A) p` over the residue field of $A$; a morphism $e_{s,A}$ from $M_{s,A}.C$ to the fibre product of $c$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathrm{residue}_A \circ \rho_A$, assumed to be an isomorphism (`hes_iso`) and compatible with the structure morphisms (`hes`). The hypothesis `hsp` asserts, for each such $A$ with algebraically closed residue field, the existence of a map $r$ from places of `modularFunctionFieldBar p` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to places of `modularFunctionFieldFullC (ResidueField A) p` over the residue field such that `IsPlaceReductionModL A p r` holds (the reduction-of-places condition of the development, namely `IsPlaceReductionAlong A (residue A) p r`) and such that $r$ computes specialisation of points: for every $A$-point $x_A$ of $X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_A$, every section $x$ of $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$ and every section $y$ of $M_{s,A}.\mathrm{toBase}$, if $x$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection is $\operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $x_A$, and $y$ followed by $e_{s,A}$ and the first projection is $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{residue}_A)$ followed by $x_A$, then $M_{s,A}.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,y = r(M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x)$.
--
--   (iii) Rational model. A curve model $M_0$ of `modularFunctionFieldFull p` over $\mathbb{Q}$, an isomorphism $e_0$ from $M_0.C$ to the fibre product of $c$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to \mathbb{Q}$, compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₀`), and the place compatibility `hcompat`: for every section $x$ of $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$, every $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $y$ of the $\mathbb{Q}$-fibre product, and every closed point $x_0$ of $M_0.C$, if $y$ followed by the first projection equals $x$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection, and if $y$ followed by $e_0^{-1}$ sends the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $x_0$, then the preimage of the valuation subring of $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$ under the composite of the right inclusion $\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldFull } p \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathtt{modularFunctionFieldFull } p$ with the base-change isomorphism onto `modularFunctionFieldBar p` equals, as a subring, the valuation subring of $M_0.\mathrm{placeOfPoint}\,x_0$.
--
--   (iv) Representability of the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$. A designation $D$ consisting of a scheme with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} R$ and a zero section; the hypothesis $hD$ that $D$ represents the subfunctor `algEquivZeroCut c ε` of the $\varepsilon$-rigidified relative Picard presheaf of $c$, i.e. that there is a rigidified line bundle (the Poincaré bundle) on the fibre product of $c$ with $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ satisfying the fibrewise condition — for every algebraically closed field $k$ and every $k$-point $s$ of the base, the restriction of the bundle to the corresponding fibre of $c$ satisfies `IsAlgEquivZero` — such that every rigidified line bundle satisfying that condition over any base $t$ is induced, up to isomorphism, along a unique $t$-point of $D$, and such that the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along the zero section is isomorphic to the unit; finally $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth (`hsm`), proper (`hpr`) and geometrically connected (`hgc`).
--
--   (v) Abel–Jacobi morphism. A morphism $\mathrm{aj} \colon X \to D$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R$, with $\varepsilon$ followed by $\mathrm{aj}$ equal to the zero section (`hajε`), and the hypothesis `haj` that $\mathrm{aj}$ classifies the expected bundle at field-valued points: for every field $K$, every morphism $t \colon \operatorname{Spec} K \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ and every $K$-point $x$ of $X$ over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{aj}$ is isomorphic to the tensor product of the line bundle (dual ideal sheaf module) of the relative effective Cartier divisor `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` attached to $x$ with the ideal sheaf module of the divisor attached to $t$ followed by $\varepsilon$.
--
--   Conclusion. With `JZero p` carrying the `HeckeAlg`-module structure `heckeModuleBar p`, where `HeckeAlg` is the polynomial ring $\mathbb{Z}[X_q : q \text{ prime}]$, there exist a bijection $\mathrm{pts}$ from `JZero p` onto the set of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and a map $\varphi$ from `HeckeAlg` to the endomorphisms of $D$ over $D.\mathrm{toBase}$, such that all of the following hold.
--
--   1.
--
--   `AbelianSchemePropertyBundle R D.toBase`: $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth and proper, the fibre over each point of $\operatorname{Spec} R$ is connected, and $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ admits a relative group law.
--
--   2.
--
--   The relative group law supplied by $hD$ for the group cut `algEquivZeroGroupCut c ε` is commutative: for every base $T$, every $t \colon T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ and all $t$-points $x, y$ of $D$, its multiplication satisfies $\mathrm{mul}\,t\,x\,y = \mathrm{mul}\,t\,y\,x$.
--
--   3.
--
--   $\mathrm{pts}$ is additive: $\mathrm{pts}(x + y) = \mathrm{mul}(\mathrm{pts}\,x, \mathrm{pts}\,y)$ for all $x, y \in$ `JZero p`.
--
--   4.
--
--   $\mathrm{pts}$ is Galois equivariant: for every $\sigma \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and $x \in$ `JZero p`, the morphism underlying $\mathrm{pts}(\sigma \cdot x)$ is $\operatorname{Spec}\sigma$ followed by the morphism underlying $\mathrm{pts}\,x$.
--
--   5.
--
--   For every valuation subring $A \subset \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $\ell$ there exist a morphism $\sigma_A \colon \operatorname{Spec} A \to \operatorname{Spec} R$, a bijection $\mathrm{pts}_A$ from `JZero p` onto the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $\sigma_A$, and a bijection $\mathrm{pts}_{\mathrm{Sp}}$ from `JZeroC (ResidueField A) p` onto the residue-field points of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{residue}_A)$ followed by $\sigma_A$, such that: $\mathrm{pts}_A\,x$ has the same underlying morphism as $\mathrm{pts}\,x$ for all $x$; $\mathrm{pts}_{\mathrm{Sp}}$ is additive for the same relative group law; and if the predicate `ReductionInputsModL A p` holds, then `ReductionOfPointsAgreesModL p A D.toBase σA ptsA ptsSp` holds, i.e. for every $x \in$ `JZero p` there is an $A$-point $x_A$ of $D$ over $\sigma_A$ whose composite with $\operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ is $\mathrm{pts}_A\,x$ and whose composite with $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{residue}_A)$ is $\mathrm{pts}_{\mathrm{Sp}}$ of the reduction `reductionModL A p x`.
--
--   6.
--
--   For every $t \in$ `HeckeAlg`: the endomorphism $\varphi(t)$ respects the group law, in the sense that for every base $T$, every $s \colon T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ and all $s$-points $x, y$ of $D$, composing $\mathrm{mul}\,s\,x\,y$ with $\varphi(t)$ equals $\mathrm{mul}\,s$ applied to the composites of $x$ and of $y$ with $\varphi(t)$; and $\mathrm{pts}(t \cdot x)$ has underlying morphism $\mathrm{pts}\,x$ followed by $\varphi(t)$ for all $x \in$ `JZero p`.
--
--   7.
--
--   $\varphi(s t) = \varphi(t)$ followed by $\varphi(s)$, for all $s, t \in$ `HeckeAlg`.
--
--   8.
--
--   $\varphi(s + t) = \mathrm{mul}$ at $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ of $\varphi(s)$ and $\varphi(t)$, for all $s, t$.
--
--   9.
--
--   $\varphi(1)$ is the identity endomorphism of $D$.
--
--   10.
--
--   $\varphi(0)$ is the neutral element of the group law at $D.\mathrm{toBase}$.
--
--   11.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi normalisation of $\mathrm{pts}$: for all sections $x, s$ of $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$ such that $s$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(R \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar p` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ whose underlying divisor is $\delta_{M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x} - \delta_{M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,s}$ and such that the morphism underlying $\mathrm{pts}$ of the class of $Dv$ is $x$ followed by $e_\eta$, the first projection and $\mathrm{aj}$.
--
--   12.
--
--   Rigidity of the Hecke action: for every prime $q$ and every endomorphism $\psi$ of $D$ over $D.\mathrm{toBase}$, if the morphism underlying $\mathrm{pts}(\mathtt{heckeOperatorBar } p\, q\, x)$ is $\mathrm{pts}\,x$ followed by $\psi$ for all $x \in$ `JZero p`, then $\varphi(\mathtt{heckeGen } q) = \psi$, where `heckeGen q` is the variable $X_q$ of `HeckeAlg`.
--
--   This is the Abel–Jacobi normalised form of the construction of the relative Jacobian of $X_0(p)$ over $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ for $\ell \nmid p$: the representing scheme $D$ of the degree-zero relative Picard functor is an abelian scheme whose $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points are identified, compatibly with the Galois action, with the Hecke action and with reduction at the places above $\ell$, with the identification pinned down by the Abel–Jacobi morphism on differences of points. It is used in the level-$p$ Jacobian interface of the development, in particular by the statements producing a level model that is a Jacobian, a points dictionary over $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$, and the dual-numbers description of the tangent space with its integral Hecke lattice.
